{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/924069-reworking-some-themes","id":924069,"title":"Reworking Some Themes","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-06-30T13:40:47-07:00","id":924069,"title":"Reworking Some Themes","content":"I've been working on a homebrew version of Werewolf: the Forsaken because while I like the system a lot and most of the fluff, there are just some themes that I'd rather handle a little differently than they are in the core. A lot of this is drawing from some ideas from Apocalypse, or just my own. I've been looking at the translation guide, but that one's made for W:tA to 1e rather than 2e, and there are some differences. Also that I'm an indecisive little baby I like to hear opinions other than my own, so here goes.<br /> <br /> I really like the theming of duality between flesh and spirit, man and wolf. But I feel like the wolf and spirit should be more separated than they are in the core (since wolves are ultimately fleshy beings). So we have two axes between man and wolf and flesh and spirit, rather than just one between man-flesh and spirit-wolf. That's just a theming shift though.<br /> <br /> My first real problem with Forsaken is only having human-born werewolves, which seems kind of silly when werewolves are from two or three worlds. I get that all the players are going to be humans which ultimately limits our own perspective, but as roleplayers we should also be able to try different perspectives if we want to, and when there are three different sides of the coin for werewolves, I'd like to be able to have werewolves be born as wolves or spirits too. There've been quite a few threads on here about the wolfborn (which are great by the way, have been a great help) as well as in the Werewolf Translation Guide. I'm just trying to refine it myself, especially for how spirit-borns... happen? It's kind of weird. Back in Apocalypse I had my own spirit breed called the Umbrid which was born from a wolf-spirit and a werewolf but I feel like that doesn't really make as much sense in the Forsaken Shadow. I was thinking maybe they'd be like the Unihar and be 'born' sometimes when werewolves get together (minus the whole parent killing thing), but I'm not sure.<br /> <br /> Anyway, so I really like the idea of balance, and while the story of Father Wolf sort of captures it, I also sort of like the triat. Not in like the sense that Apocalypse handles it where everything's sort of out of control with two in too much power, but rather a sort of shifting balances between the three? I can also see some parallels with like the 'Wyld' (creation) being associated with the Hisil and Mother Luna, 'Weaver' (order/stasis) with humanity and the world of flesh, the gauntlet, and 'Wyrm' (balance/destruction) being associated with Father Wolf (destroying things that disturb the balance), primal order. I'd like to try to rework my own origin story with the three forces (possibly three parents?!) for the Uratha, but I'm not 100% sure how to go about it.<br /> <br /> This is probably really rambling and silly, so I'll stop here. I'd just like to see some other ideas about how to make this work and stuff! Thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3094-leafy","name":"Leafy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3094_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-30T15:16:22-07:00","id":924127,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm just trying to refine it myself, especially for how spirit-borns... happen? </div> </div> </div>I may be misremembering, but there kinda are spirit wolves related to Father Wolf already. It wouldn't take a great deal of imagination to alter them into sort of reverse werewolves.<br /> <br /> Plus, remember human werewolves aren't exactly born either. Luna chooses you to some degree, or she's capable of doing that if she pleases.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Anyway, so I really like the idea of balance, and while the story of Father Wolf sort of captures it, I also sort of like the triat. Not in like the sense that Apocalypse handles it where everything's sort of out of control with two in too much power, but rather a sort of shifting balances between the three? I can also see some parallels with like the 'Wyld' (creation) being associated with the Hisil and Mother Luna, 'Weaver' (order/stasis) with humanity and the world of flesh, the gauntlet, and 'Wyrm' (balance/destruction) being associated with Father Wolf (destroying things that disturb the balance), primal order. I'd like to try to rework my own origin story with the three forces (possibly three parents?!) for the Uratha, but I'm not 100% sure how to go about it. </div> </div> </div>I can't remember where, but somewhere in Forsaken, there's a section that comments on Helios, Luna and Father Wolf acting as a triad of sorts, cycling the Hisil.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-30T15:25:53-07:00","id":924130,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3094-leafy\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3094_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3094\" data-vbnamecard=\"3094\">Leafy</a><br /> <br /> First, thank you for posting. You are not silly, so don't be worried about that. I'd point to Skinchangers, specifically the wolf spirit that is a skinchanger using the rules presented in that book. I think those concepts will help with your work.<br /> <br /> In my own setting I don't make Minor and Major Templates exclusive, which is to say each soul has a slot for a Minor template and a Major Template, and if they have both I call that a Complete Template. I count Blood Lines, Lodges, Entitlements, Legacies as Minor Templates for purposes of taking up slots. Anyway it has been testing really well and for your game I'd just have a wolf spirit be required to be transformed into a Skinchanger (with the basic human stat block based on their first stolen skin) before they qualify for the Werewolf template.<br /> <br /> I am interested in seeing what you come up with. Be the change you want to see in the world.<br /> <br /> Good luck!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-30T15:25:53-07:00","id":924131,"title":"","content":"Trying to come at this just with the changes you want rather than commenting on the Forsaken game as is.<br /> <br /> For a werewolf spirit, you could use the wolf hosts from Night Horrors and, once they've developed enough soul shards can bind with a person or form a living structure of their own.<br /> <br /> For your Triat, you will probably want Helios as more of a form of oppressive order, and the Maeljin are an easy stand-in for the Wyrm.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-30T15:32:55-07:00","id":924139,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/924069-reworking-some-themes?p=924131#post924131\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For your Triat, you will probably want Helios as more of a form of oppressive order, and the Maeljin are an easy stand-in for the Wyrm.</div> </div> </div> </div>Also you could just bring to the forefront the Hostts already in the Game between the Azlu/Spinning Hag and Bishilu/Plague King reforming for the fight-against-Weaver-Wyrm conflict from apocalypse. Changing focus is easier than crafting whole-cloth.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":3279,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","user":"Pale_Crusader","date":". 06-30-2016, 06:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-30T15:53:32-07:00","id":924149,"title":"","content":"Aaah, thank you all for responding!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/924069-reworking-some-themes?p=924127#post924127\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I may be misremembering, but there kinda are spirit wolves related to Father Wolf already. It wouldn't take a great deal of imagination to alter them into sort of reverse werewolves.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a really interesting concept I hadn't considered! But what I'm going for is kinda Wolf daddy, Mother Luna, and some other force all coming together to make the Uratha for some reason or another, but then if regular humans can be 'blessed' with wolf-bloodedness and stuff, it's possible something like this could work.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pale_Crusader</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">First, thank you for posting. You are not silly, so don't be worried about that. I'd point to Skinchangers, specifically the wolf spirit that is a skinchanger using the rules presented in that book. I think those concepts will help with your work.<br /> <br /> In my own setting I don't make Minor and Major Templates exclusive, which is to say each soul has a slot for a Minor template and a Major Template, and if they have both I call that a Complete Template. I count Blood Lines, Lodges, Entitlements, Legacies as Minor Templates for purposes of taking up slots. Anyway it has been testing really well and for your game I'd just have a wolf spirit be required to be transformed into a Skinchanger (with the basic human stat block based on their first stolen skin) before they qualify for the Werewolf template.<br /> <br /> I am interested in seeing what you come up with. Be the change you want to see in the world.<br /> <br /> Good luck!</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for being so nice! I've not read about Skinchangers at all yet, so I'll definitely look into that too.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">For a werewolf spirit, you could use the wolf hosts from Night Horrors and, once they've developed enough soul shards can bind with a person or form a living structure of their own.<br /> <br /> For your Triat, you will probably want Helios as more of a form of oppressive order, and the Maeljin are an easy stand-in for the Wyrm.</div> </div> </div> </div>Another interesting idea! I was actually thinking they'd just pop into the flesh world with a form of their own, similar to how werewolves who were born human can just show up into the spirit world with a spirit form. I was thinking more along their 'life cycle' as a spirit, like are they marked by Lunes/Luna at a certain point? At what level of consciousness does a spirit become able to transform into a werewolf?<br /> <br /> Helios is a cool idea for an order spirit. I did read that bit with the Moon, Sun, and Wolf in the core, but it never occured to me to have him be the order guy. It definitely makes sense with how unchanging and firm he is though. Do you guys think it would make sense for him to offer the form of a human or just the flesh body in general for the Uratha though?<br /> <br /> As for the Maeljin and stuff, I'd not like the 'Wyrm' role necessarily be decided as just 'evil' or 'destructive', what I'm going for is keeper of balance (by destroying those who upset it). The triat will pretty much be the three parents of the Uratha, so I think Father Wolf fits that role best. If anything, I'd say Maeljin would be an example of Destroyer being out of balance, in the same way Idigam might be seen as Creation being out of balance.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pale_Crusader</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also you could just bring to the forefront the Host conflicts already in the Game between the Azlu/Spinning Hag and Bishilu/Plague King reforming for fight against Weaver-Wyrm conflict.</div> </div> </div> </div>This! Yeah, I was considering having them be sort of agents for the triat becoming slightly out of balance, though Bishilu might also be alignable with just the Hisil/Creation spirit since they're opening the Gauntlet more?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3094-leafy","name":"Leafy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3094_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-30T16:47:47-07:00","id":924170,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leafy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/924069-reworking-some-themes?p=924149#post924149\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another interesting idea! I was actually thinking they'd just pop into the flesh world with a form of their own, similar to how werewolves who were born human can just show up into the spirit world with a spirit form. I was thinking more along their 'life cycle' as a spirit, like are they marked by Lunes/Luna at a certain point? At what level of consciousness does a spirit become able to transform into a werewolf?</div> </div> </div> </div>Likely Rank 3, which is when they develop an individual personality beyond 'hungerer for Essence.'<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Helios is a cool idea for an order spirit. I did read that bit with the Moon, Sun, and Wolf in the core, but it never occured to me to have him be the order guy. It definitely makes sense with how unchanging and firm he is though. Do you guys think it would make sense for him to offer the form of a human or just the flesh body in general for the Uratha though? </div> </div> </div>He's kind of annoyed at werewolves in Forsaken as is, though you could change that of course. In killing Urfarah and destroying Pangaea, they made it so Helios' presence drives spirits from the Hisil to the Gurihal, the real world, as opposed to a safer boundary between. In-game he has a race of bullmen worshipers detailed in War Against the Pure.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for the Maeljin and stuff, I'd not like the 'Wyrm' role necessarily be decided as just 'evil' or 'destructive', what I'm going for is keeper of balance (by destroying those who upset it). The triat will pretty much be the three parents of the Uratha, so I think Father Wolf fits that role best. If anything, I'd say Maeljin would be an example of Destroyer being out of balance, in the same way Idigam might be seen as Creation being out of balance. </div> </div> </div>Okay. The Maeljin are definitely depicted as an evil force and not a natural one. Though Father Wolf is, at least in the setting, long dead.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 06-30-2016, 07:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-30T17:17:59-07:00","id":924187,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leafy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/924069-reworking-some-themes#post924069\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've been working on a homebrew version of Werewolf: the Forsaken because while I like the system a lot and most of the fluff, there are just some themes that I'd rather handle a little differently than they are in the core. A lot of this is drawing from some ideas from Apocalypse, or just my own. I've been looking at the translation guide, but that one's made for W:tA to 1e rather than 2e, and there are some differences. Also that I'm an indecisive little baby I like to hear opinions other than my own, so here goes.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hey, that's what forums are for! Always happy to help.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leafy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/924069-reworking-some-themes#post924069\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I really like the theming of duality between flesh and spirit, man and wolf. But I feel like the wolf and spirit should be more separated than they are in the core (since wolves are ultimately fleshy beings). So we have two axes between man and wolf and flesh and spirit, rather than just one between man-flesh and spirit-wolf. That's just a theming shift though.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you may be somewhat misunderstanding the intent. Harmony is meant to represent duality between flesh werewolf and spirit werewolf. If you look at the Breaking Points, neither direction is really inherently tied to wolf-like or human-like behavior. A werewolf that spends her whole life living as a wolf in the wilderness but never enters the Shadow (which would make sense, as it's really not something wolves typically do), she'll quickly end up with Harmony 10. Conversely, a werewolf who never leaves human form, but spends the majority of his time in the Shadow will quickly fall to Harmony 0. The thing I think you have hit on here though, is that that the game measures spirit/flesh duality, but not really human/wolf duality. That's by design, as one of the very intentional statements Forsaken 2e makes is that the Uratha are not humans that can turn into wolves, or wolves that can turn into humans. They are shapeshifters that can take the shape of humans and wolves. They don't really have wolf natures or human natures to balance, their nature is all-werewolf. Now, if you feel that particular duality is lacking in the game, you could certainly add a second axis to Harmony, although you'll have to consider what the effects of the extremes of this axis would be and what kind of actions would cause Breaking Points in which direction.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leafy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/924069-reworking-some-themes#post924069\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My first real problem with Forsaken is only having human-born werewolves, which seems kind of silly when werewolves are from two or three worlds. I get that all the players are going to be humans which ultimately limits our own perspective, but as roleplayers we should also be able to try different perspectives if we want to, and when there are three different sides of the coin for werewolves, I'd like to be able to have werewolves be born as wolves or spirits too. There've been quite a few threads on here about the wolfborn (which are great by the way, have been a great help) as well as in the Werewolf Translation Guide. I'm just trying to refine it myself, especially for how spirit-borns... happen? It's kind of weird. Back in Apocalypse I had my own spirit breed called the Umbrid which was born from a wolf-spirit and a werewolf but I feel like that doesn't really make as much sense in the Forsaken Shadow. I was thinking maybe they'd be like the Unihar and be 'born' sometimes when werewolves get together (minus the whole parent killing thing), but I'm not sure.</div> </div> </div> </div>There have been a couple of threads brainstorming wolf-born werewolves. I'm a big fan of <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/922891-born-of-wolves\">this one</a><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leafy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/924069-reworking-some-themes#post924069\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Anyway, so I really like the idea of balance, and while the story of Father Wolf sort of captures it, I also sort of like the triat. Not in like the sense that Apocalypse handles it where everything's sort of out of control with two in too much power, but rather a sort of shifting balances between the three? I can also see some parallels with like the 'Wyld' (creation) being associated with the Hisil and Mother Luna, 'Weaver' (order/stasis) with humanity and the world of flesh, the gauntlet, and 'Wyrm' (balance/destruction) being associated with Father Wolf (destroying things that disturb the balance), primal order. I'd like to try to rework my own origin story with the three forces (possibly three parents?!) for the Uratha, but I'm not 100% sure how to go about it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know too much about Apocalypse myself, but <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/109-claire-redfield\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar109_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"109\" data-vbnamecard=\"109\">Claire Redfield</a> has done some really great Forsacalypse homebrew that I enjoyed even as someone with only vague knowledge of Apocalypse. <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/848028-mystery-cult-pentech-worldwide-leaders-in-natural-technology\">Her take on Pentex</a> was my favorite, but it sounds like you might be more interested in her <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/809400-umaraha-e-the-children-of-the-worm\">Cult of the Wyrm</a> writeup.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leafy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/924069-reworking-some-themes#post924069\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is probably really rambling and silly, so I'll stop here. I'd just like to see some other ideas about how to make this work and stuff! Thanks!</div> </div> </div> </div>Not at all, a lot of these are all pretty common desires expressed by Forsaken fans. And even if it were, again, that's what forums are for <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 06-30-2016, 08:34 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-30T19:47:22-07:00","id":924247,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/924069-reworking-some-themes?p=924187#post924187\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you may be somewhat misunderstanding the intent. Harmony is meant to represent duality between flesh werewolf and spirit werewolf. If you look at the Breaking Points, neither direction is really inherently tied to wolf-like or human-like behavior. A werewolf that spends her whole life living as a wolf in the wilderness but never enters the Shadow (which would make sense, as it's really not something wolves typically do), she'll quickly end up with Harmony 10. Conversely, a werewolf who never leaves human form, but spends the majority of his time in the Shadow will quickly fall to Harmony 0. The thing I think you have hit on here though, is that that the game measures spirit/flesh duality, but not really human/wolf duality. That's by design, as one of the very intentional statements Forsaken 2e makes is that the Uratha are not humans that can turn into wolves, or wolves that can turn into humans. They are shapeshifters that can take the shape of humans and wolves. They don't really have wolf natures or human natures to balance, their nature is all-werewolf. Now, if you feel that particular duality is lacking in the game, you could certainly add a second axis to Harmony, although you'll have to consider what the effects of the extremes of this axis would be and what kind of actions would cause Breaking Points in which direction.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I like Harmony as it is actually. Werewolves aren't wolves or men, but they aren't flesh or spirit either. I like that as it is. I don't necessarily feel like I need another axis of harmony, I was just using the two axes as way to separate human from flesh and wolf from spirit. Another sort of harmony meter is an interesting idea, but I feel like that might complicate gameplay. The only way I might be able to see that is possibly between Instinct and Humanity which might be an interesting way to look at it? Too far into wolfy things and you start to suffer social penalties with humans and the other way vice verca? I'm not exactly sure how else to penalize either side since I feel like the biggest difference would be how the character would think? I'd have to give it some thought.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't know too much about Apocalypse myself, but <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/109-claire-redfield\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar109_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"109\" data-vbnamecard=\"109\">Claire Redfield</a> has done some really great Forsacalypse homebrew that I enjoyed even as someone with only vague knowledge of Apocalypse. <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/848028-mystery-cult-pentech-worldwide-leaders-in-natural-technology\">Her take on Pentex</a> was my favorite, but it sounds like you might be more interested in her <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/809400-umaraha-e-the-children-of-the-worm\">Cult of the Wyrm</a> writeup.</div> </div> </div> </div>Those are both really great writeups! I probably wouldn't use either myself though, since I'd ultimately not be using the exact same triat as Apocalypse. I'd prefer not to demonize the 'Wyrm' too much and rather have each side having the *potential* to get out of control, but not necessarily one or another great threat. Ideally, all three should be in a relative balance, and the Uratha's job is to keep things from getting out of control.<br /> <br /> For example, in this setting the Idigam might not have been banished to the moon (probably). Rather, they're simply <i>from</i> it, since the moon, with her shifting faces, represents the changeable 'wyld', infinite potential, change and creation. The idigam are that creation gone without purpose, without a true form or place, becoming 'Wyld' gone wrong. In that same sense, there can be plenty of examples of things that take the ideas of destruction and decay out of balance and its place in the triat, but the 'wyrm' itself is a necessary and important part of the triat, and in fact might be closest to the Uratha in purpose. After all, they get rid of most things that disturb the balance by killing (i.e, destroying) them.<br /> <br /> In the same way that too much flesh and too much spirit is a bad thing, both are necessary to a werewolf's nature. I'd like the idea of the triat to be the same thing, the idea of everything teetering just enough on balance to stay under control, but precarious enough that control might collapse.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3094,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3094-leafy","user":"Leafy","date":". 06-30-2016, 10:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3094-leafy","name":"Leafy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3094_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-01T00:29:19-07:00","id":924334,"title":"","content":"Okay guys, I figured I'd try my hand at a first draft sort of write-up, just to get a kind of sense what I'm going for here? Revisions and critiques are more than welcome. ^^&quot;<br /> <br /> <i>Most Uratha agree that creation began with three great spirits, the Shalas-Maguralath, as they are called in the First Tongue. Iduth, the moon with her shifting faces was the spirit of creation, spawned endless new spirits, concepts, creatures, ever-changing in form, not yet given purpose or place. This would be granted by Helios (?), the steadfast, unchanging sun, who imposed order upon creation, giving his sister's creations a place in the young world. However, the world grew full with the children of the Moon and the Sun, unable to sustain the creations of the two. When things grew out of control, the alpha predator, Urfarah stood up to the task. The strongest, most intelligent and steadfast of the hunter spirits, he would pursue those who burdened creation. The old, the infirm, and those who had no place, all were torn down by Father Wolf. This was how balance was maintained.<br /> <br /> The People were born when creation was still young, in the days where the Border Marches ran thin, the days where flesh could walk the world of the spirits and spirits could drink the essence in the lands of flesh. Some time had passed, and as the great three spirits continued their duties they found resentment growing between each other, leading to squabbles and disagreements. &quot;It's not fair for you to destroy our creations!&quot; the Sun and Moon would complain. Father Wolf would only say, &quot;Perhaps I would not have to, were there not so many.&quot; These fights eventually began to cause disturbances in the balance, with Luna refusing to allow some of her creations to be given a form, lest their perfection be ruined and they become vulnerable to destruction. Helios would solidify the Gauntlet, his heavy, powerful rays choking out both the Hisil and the physical world. Even Father Wolf would kill without reason, attempting to preempt the other two. From these battles, all of creation would suffer as the balance shifted uncontrollably.<br /> <br /> One day, a truce was begged for by the beings of both flesh and spirit, led by a child of Helios called Man, a being of flesh. Though puny and weak compared to the great spirits, they possessed an intellect above most creation and a gift for refinement that caught the eye of the Sun, who heeded their call and gathered the other two for a council. It was soon realized that the three would be unable to see clearly how to find balance, as each had become so focused on their own role, though it was plain even for them to see that the world was suffering for their lack of it.<br /> <br /> The Shalas-Maguralath decided to create a being who would find balance for them and to maintain it, something gifted by the three of them, together. Helios called upon a human, Urfarah brought a wolf, and Luna, a Lune. The three were brought together, then bound, the three children of the spirits to become one. Luna said unto the three, &quot;I give you the gift of Change, to become what you must to restore balance to our world.&quot;<br /> &quot;I give you the gift of Harmony, that you may remember your own place in the world, as well as minding that of others,&quot; said Helios.<br /> &quot;I give you Teeth, child,&quot; said Father Wolf, &quot;That you may hunt those who break the balance between us.&quot;<br /> <br /> By the power of the Shalas-Maguralath, the three creatures became one, an Uratha -- part man, part wolf, part spirit. And it became their duty, and all those who came after to maintain the balance of the three spirits. Ours is to hunt those who wander where they do not belong, who disrupt the natural order, who kill without purpose, who take no form. This is our place. These are our prey.</i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3094-leafy","name":"Leafy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3094_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-01T08:28:54-07:00","id":924469,"title":"","content":"Very cool. I especially like that Wolf's job was to cull the weak and infirm, it makes it all the more clear why it was so important for the First Pack to kill it when it became too weak to carry out the hunt itself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-02T21:46:10-07:00","id":925200,"title":"","content":"Thank you, Charlaquin! Got my gears turning a bit and decided to continue with the 'myth' a bit. Mind you, I'm not 100% sure if I want to use the tribes as they are, so the Firstborn and stuff may not be the same, but for now, I'm gonna roll with Urfarah dying.<br /> <br /> <i>For a while after the birth of the Uratha, things were good in Pangaea. Things came to be in balance once more, with the help of the shapechangers who, under the tutelage of Father Wolf and his brood of spirits, became adept hunters of their own right, slaying and binding and bargaining to keep everything in its proper place.<br /> <br /> As time went on, the pack swelled in size, but the Alpha, Urfarah, grew weak. No longer was he the mighty hunter who took down his prey without fail. With his failure to kill the Beshilu and Azlu, among others, the balance was no longer stable without the force of destruction at his former strength. The Firstborn, the sons and daughters of Urfarah knew what must be done. The weak and infirm were a burden unto creation, and if Father Wolf was no longer fit to rule, they must take his place. They, with the help of their half-siblings, the Uratha, would restore the balance.<br /> <br /> Though a mighty spirit, Urfarah was weakened, and what was more, as a spirit, he had a ban to not defend himself against a killing blow if challenged by those who could replace him. His bane was his children's teeth, the same teeth he gave to the Uratha when they were first born. He was slain that night at the fangs of his children, releasing a terrible howl upon his death, a cry that reached all the way up to the heavens.<br /> <br /> Luna and Helios both saw, they both heard the crime of the Wolf's children. Luna turned her face on them, cursing them to burn at the touch of silver and with madness. Helios' powerful rays seared the Border Marches, warping them into a solidified mass, the Gauntlet sundering spirit from flesh.<br /> <br /> After their murder, the Firstborn and the Uratha repented by continuing their father's duty to continue to maintain balance in his place, the Firstborn taking up the biggest mantle as the forces of destroyer and the Uratha doing what they could in addition to their task as balance-keepers. They took up the Oath of the Moon and through their efforts have gained some form of forgiveness, with Luna continuing her patronage. Helios, however, has completely rejected the Uratha. Set in his ways, as always, he continues his duty, and even without his patronage, we will still continue ours.</i><br /> <br /> ----------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> While I was at it, I did a rough thing for the breeds too! Although I'm not too sure if they're balanced in terms of actual gameplay systems. I'll mark the systems in red.<br /> <br /> <i>Before the Sundering, the Hishu, Urhanu, and Uralathu were about perfectly even in numbers, each consisting of 1/3 of the total werewolf population. After the Sundering, however, the Uralathu became rather scarce, and Ulle'u began to be observed. In the modern era, Hishu are most common, followed by the Urhanu, Ulle'u, and Uralathu in that order.</i><br /> <br /> <b>Hishu</b><br /> Born of humans in the world of flesh. Maybe they have a werewolf parent. Maybe they don't. Regardless, they're werewolves now.<span style=\"color:#B22222\"> They're the same starting as they are in the core. 7 Harmony, no special perks or problems.</span> They're easily the most common.<br /> <br /> <b>Urhanu</b><br /> Urhanu were born as wolves to wolves. Much like how the human born werewolves before their first change find themselves with stronger instincts and senses, so too are Urhanu burdened with an intellect that their pack mates can't seem to understand. This, along with the glimpses into the Hisil may lead a young Urhanu to believe something is 'wrong' with herself, or maybe the world around her. Things become much clearer with the change, however, though they have much to learn in both the ways of the spirits and of humans. <span style=\"color:#B22222\">An Urhanu cannot choose Mental Skills to be primary at character creation. They begin play with the Persistent Condition <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/840066-breeds-in-forsaken?p=849616#post849616\">'Raised By Wolves'</a>. Urhanu start with Harmony 7 and Primal Urge 2.</span><br /> <br /> <b>Uralathu (name pending and sort of a misnomer)</b><br /> The Uralathu begin their lives as spirits, usually wolf-spirits*. No one's exactly sure what triggers them to change, if it's something chosen when they first form, some trigger later on, or something else, but most Ithaeur agree it is a change they are chosen to undertake by Luna. They're scarce and have been since the Sundering. Whether this is because of Luna's remaining grudge against the Uratha or the difficulty now for spirits to pass the barrier, it is uncertain. Most often during the First Change, Uralathu find themselves tearing their way out of the spirit world and are even more bewildered to find themselves to have flesh on the other side. <span style=\"color:#B22222\">They begin with 3 Harmony and on account of this must contend with a Ban they had, possibly related to their role as a spirit but not necessarily. However, thanks to their natural affinity with the world of spirits, it is easier for them to cross the border back, treating it as a rote action.</span><br /> *Other spirits have been known to change. Mother Luna isn't exactly known for being picky and indeed, her whims have marked some strange candidates for the change. It is easiest for wolf spirits, as they are already a part of Wolf's brood, it is more natural for them to take the role of the predator to maintain the balance. For some, related spirits, such as other predator spirits like bears, the transformation is not so challenging, as their role is not so different, but the change for some, like it might be for a pain spirit or perhaps even a lune, is dangerous not just for the spirit changing, but also werewolves. Uralathu do remember much of their time as spirits and tend to carry over old quirks. While an Uralathu born as a Lune may be seen as a portentous gift from Luna, their madness can result in an unpredictable Uratha, unable to keep its role to maintain the balance. In addition, the change is traumatic and dangerous, more so for the spirits than any others. For those without a form like one of the five the Uratha take, the change is very radical and often described as painful. Most never are able to take their old form again. Some adapt. Most die. The Gauntlet is also a daunting thing to cross, and the journey becomes more and more challenging where it is thick, such as in cities. As a result, most surviving Uralathu are from the wilderness and are nature spirits (again, mostly wolf-spirits).<br /> <br /> <b>Ulle'u</b><br /> On the off chance two werewolves happen to have a child together, that child is almost invariably a werewolf. However, those born to werewolves lead more challenging lives than most, even for werewolves. All Ulle'u are deformed in some sense. There are three leading theories as to why this is the case. The first camp contends that because all Uratha are children of the same parents, the pairing can be likened to spiritual incest, resulting in the deformities. Others believe that being half-man, half-wolf, half-spirit, werewolves are just naturally 'too much' in one body. The child is essentially overloaded and is unable to bear all that they are in their bodies, leading to malformation. The third theory is similar to the third, but rather relate it to the curse from Luna. Normal werewolves have the curse in their body which sometimes is passed to their children, but having two werewolves for parents results in a child who is doubly cursed, which shows in its body.<br /> Whatever the case, Ulle'u have been a part of werewolf society since at least after the Sundering. Their existence isn't necessarily against any laws, but it's still considered an ethical question of whether or not werewolves should have children together, considering their deformities. Regardless, they are valued by most as any werewolf and tend to be better at being what they are than most, especially newly changed. They are born either in Hishu or Urhanu form (dependent on the mother's) and don't ordinarily shift until their first change which is around puberty. However, strong emotions can cause accidental changes to the neighboring form of the one they're in. Being raised (ideally) by two werewolves and knowing what they are gives the Ulle'u a great advantage, and <span style=\"color:#B22222\">having this upbringing makes them better able to maintain a balanced Harmony. If they have a breaking point toward Spirit, they only subtract one die for each point of Harmony below 4 instead of the usual 5, and toward Flesh they are penalized for each point above 6. They begin play with 5 Harmony, as well as the Persistent Condition that marks their deformity. Most aren't able to be resolved.</span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3094,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3094-leafy","user":"Leafy","date":".  updated Ulle'u perk 07-04-2016, 07:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3094-leafy","name":"Leafy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3094_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-03T23:43:38-07:00","id":925762,"title":"","content":"Shapeshifting no longer needs a roll, at least in 2nd edition, so you may need to alter the benefit of Ulle'u a bit.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-04T00:27:14-07:00","id":925775,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thorbes</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/924069-reworking-some-themes?p=925762#post925762\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Shapeshifting no longer needs a roll, at least in 2nd edition, so you may need to alter the benefit of Ulle'u a bit.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oooh, thank you. You can tell how rusty I am on some things still, haha. <br /> Hmm, but then what should I do instead..? I could just expand the threshold at which they're in pretty much full control of their form (3, 4, 5, 6, 7) or... yeah, I'm not sure.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3094-leafy","name":"Leafy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3094_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-04T01:46:10-07:00","id":925790,"title":"","content":"That was my first thought too. Consider their Harmony ever slighty closer to 5 or something.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-04T11:46:54-07:00","id":925997,"title":"","content":"Alternatively, they could just be a bit better at maintaining a balanced harmony. If they're having a breaking point towards Spirit, they subtract one die for each point of Harmony below 4, and toward Flesh they subtract one die for each point above 6. This rule would, of course, replace the rule for other werewolves where they subtract dice based on their distance from 5. That way it's just a little bit easier for them to not get flung to the extremes of Harmony. Since they're possibly the werewolf-iest werewolves, it would make sense for them to just be more balanced than those who grew up as something else.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3094-leafy","name":"Leafy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3094_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-04T13:41:27-07:00","id":926058,"title":"","content":"That certainly works. I'm thinking... shouldn't perhaps the human born have somthing special too? The 4 options should be balanced with each other.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-04T16:07:37-07:00","id":926128,"title":"","content":"That was something I was concerned about, but I gave humans no penalty either, so I thought that might balance it. Although really, one could argue the Spiritborn really have about the same start in terms of distance from 5 Harmony. Their ban would go away if they advanced more in Harmony. I guess if anything, though, I'd rather penalize the Uralathu more than give the Hishu a perk, unless I came up with a penalty for them too. Additionally, the only advantage I really gave the wolfborn was the extra dot in Primal Urge which I'm not sure is necessarily what I want?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3094,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3094-leafy","user":"Leafy","date":". 07-04-2016, 07:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3094-leafy","name":"Leafy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3094_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-05T02:17:37-07:00","id":926331,"title":"","content":"I consider Conditions to be an advantage even when negative.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-05T20:24:48-07:00","id":926808,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thorbes</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/924069-reworking-some-themes?p=926331#post926331\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I consider Conditions to be an advantage even when negative.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hm, I assume you mean because of the Beats you can rack up. Well, I'd rather not give Hishus more advantages to make up for the others being too advantaged. Then it'd just be Werewolf: the Forsaken Except Now You Have a Couple More Advantages. In that case, I'd just nerf the others harder.<br /> <br /> For Urhanu, I think it's fine as it is actually. They don't start with the native language for free, they'd have to buy a dot of the Language merit for that, and they can't start with Mental Skills as their primary. I think that's enough of a starting penalty to have an extra dot of Primal Urge (although maybe I'm wrong and again, I'm not really happy with this as their boon). Their Condition is one that they start with and can eventually get resolved. I think it's balanced fine as is with the Hishu, though feel free to offer alternatives.<br /> <br /> Ulle'u's boost comes from their more balanced Harmony, and they start with a permanent condition that deforms them in some way. Both of these would last throughout the entire game. Easy enough fix, you just don't get Beats for their Condition. Consider the Harmony boost the replacement for that.<br /> <br /> And for Uralathu.... I don't know if starting at a lower harmony is really a disadvantage enough. Like yeah, you have a Ban, but it's the same distance from 5 as 7. You can eventually lose it. Plus, they have the ability to get into the Hisil easier. I have two ideas for a solution to this:<br /> 1. As easy as it is for them to slip into the Shadow, the Uralathu find themselves having a harder time leaving. -2 modifier on rolls to enter the Flesh.<br /> 2. The spiritborn struggle to adapt to the ways of the Flesh. It just doesn't stick with them as easily. They gain an extra die on every roll on breaking points toward Flesh.<br /> <br /> also considering naming my Spiritborn something more appropriate like Ulludib (bootleg sumerian translation meaning 'those that cross', after their habit of breaching the gauntlet upon changing and their affinity for it as werewolves).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3094,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3094-leafy","user":"Leafy","date":". 07-05-2016, 11:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3094-leafy","name":"Leafy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3094_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-17T02:51:47-07:00","id":988754,"title":"","content":"Sorry to go gravedigging, but I'm afraid I'm gonna need this thread again, as I'm actually going to be trying to run a game with this homebrew very soon, so I need things at least in somewhat of a functional order before we start the game. They'll be starting as cubs, not yet a part of any tribe, so the players will be experiencing their introductions to the new tribes both as characters and as players. While we're on that topic, this is what I have at the moment.<br /> <br /> I'm planning on having 10 Playable Tribes, two for each aspect of Renown, each dedicated to their own ideals and sacred hunt. I'm not entirely sure if I should have it devoted to prey, as I'd need to find a way to divide up the prey into more specific things probably, but I'd be happy to hear alternatives as well. However, the fallen tribes are already complete!<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> There are three Fallen Tribes, one for each part of the triad. Each tribe is quite large in comparison to the others, each having both new initiates and fallen members from the other 10 tribes. However, due to their aims conflicting with not only each other, but also the playable tribes, their power is not very consolidated, with each 'tribe' for itself. However, they have many allies both in the hisil and the flesh, and can be dangerous foes regardless.<br /> <br /> <b>Moon-Touched</b><br /> <i>Hoping vainly to appease their mother, they sacrifice their own sanity and stability of form to protect their mother's beautiful creations. They ally themselves strongly with lunes, and have more spirit-born members than any other tribe, fallen or otherwise. They aren't often seen, as they often are fortified in the deepest, most mysterious reaches of the Hisil, only rarely treading on flesh's lands, but when they do, they wreak havoc, devastating the gauntlet and spreading chaos in their wake.<br /> Due to their near-universal madness, it is difficult for outsiders to comprehend their motives. They seem to follow their own whims, and those of their closest allies, the many spirits of the Hisil, in particular Lunes. They have a close working relationship with the Beshilu, who also strive to tear down the gauntlet.<br /> It is believed that in the days of old, before humans had spread the solidification of the gauntlet beyond what Helios had already managed, the Moon-Touched were much more widespread, tearing open the gauntlet into massive, gaping wounds, being only held in check not only by the Uratha, but also their fallen counterparts. These days, however, they seem to be more on the retreat, only occasionally moving on the offensive, often directing their attacks in places they can cause the most havoc, often spreading mass lunacy in the hearts of cities by doing so. For them, Lunacy is a sacred part of their forms, to spread the insights they've found in their own madness, and sometimes, to even spread their curse. Indeed, many of their numbers not directly born from spirits are Uratha and wolf-blooded created from such raids (which ironically, can create their greatest enemies as well).<br /> They are patronized by Silver Wolf, one of the two Firstborn who abstained from hunting Urfarah. It is said she had an important role in convincing Luna to offer the rest of the Uratha some form of amnesty, however, her brood were the only ones completely exempt from her punishment, being completely unphased by the presence of silver, and indeed, use it often in the name of their mother. However, Silver Wolf and the Moon-Touched proved an object lesson for the rest of the Uratha, as their close relationship with the Lunes kept them from their duty to protect the balance and eventually caused the insanity that now permeates their tribe. Silver Wolf is now known to the rest of the tribes as the Mad Wolf, and few of the Uratha outside of her own tribe can recall her true name, or her previous aid.</i><br /> <br /> <b>The Crowned Ones</b><br /> <i>The Uratha are almost universally shunned by their father the Sun, with one exception-- the Crowned Ones, children of the Sky King himself. Populated mostly by the sons and daughters of Helios, the human-born, they are the bringers of order and control. Unlike the Moon-Touched or the (OTHER TRIBE), they operate more silently. Their battles are not fought with tooth and claw, but with cash and cutting wit, bringing the world to a pristine, bright, and pure order right underneath the noses of the other tribes. When they can help it, they prefer to avoid the other Uratha, as they hold them in disdain, as unclean, messy things unfit to rule. However, their ideals for the world are directly opposed to the Uratha's and when they are able to, they will capture, drive out, or put down their cousins in much the fashion they would to a stray, rabid dog.<br /> They take a scientific, clinical view of the Hisil, as a threat to the world they call home, having observed the danger many spirits can wreak. To them, the way to ensure the safety of both sides is to solidify the gauntlet, that none may enter or exit. Inevitably, this stifles and chokes out the life on both sides, yet in the eyes of the Crowned Ones, this is good: a pure slate, wiped clean, on which they can create a utopian society alit by the rays of the sun.<br /> They do know how to use spirits to their advantage, aware that not all are a threat to their cause. Helions, of course, are an obvious ally, but other spirits include those of technology and human origin (provided they can be brought to heel) and lastly, the Azlu and their spider kin, are closely allied with the Crowned Ones, both working together to solidify the gauntlet. However, tribal culture often dictates using spirits more as tools than allies.<br /> It is said that their patron, August Wolf, was one of the two Firstborn who refused to hunt Urfarah. While Silver Wolf fled to the deep Hisil, howling her grief to the stars, August Wolf remained witness to the shame of his brothers and sisters, and some believe that it is he who first brought the word of his father's death to Helios, disgusted by his murderous siblings. His own nobility in the face of the crimes of his siblings, according to the Crowned Ones anyway, allowed him and his brood the continued favour of Helios, and they continue to carry out his will to this day. To the other tribes, August Wolf is now known as Ice Wolf, for his cold indifference, inaction, and betrayal of his own siblings, and for the cold touch that sears the lands under the domain of their sun.</i><br /> <br /> <b>The Destroyer's Blood</b><br /> <i>Though all the Uratha carry the sins of the murder of their father, and all continue his duty, none take this role more seriously than the Destroyer's Blood, to the point of severe fault. To them, the forces of creation are already beyond control, beyond redemption. The only thing left to do is do as what they claim Father would have done. Destroy, devour, burn it to the ground.<br /> So it was back when the Destroyer's Blood first began, the Uratha under the banner of Bloodied Wolf, the strongest and most fearless of Urfarah's get. Bloodied Wolf would sink his teeth in his prey and never let go, even if it may mean his death, for he knew the purity of the hunt would only be found in destruction, be it of himself, or his quarry. Almost all legends agree, both within the tribe and without, that it was Bloodied Wolf who led the decision to slay Father Wolf when his time came, and it is said that it was his teeth that found Urfarah's throat at his last breath. As the new leader of the forces of destruction, he led his brothers and sisters on the warpath, to subdue the onslaught of maddened creation and stifling order.<br /> Perhaps that is their claim, but in truth, the tribe of today is now under new management. No one has heard from Bloodied Wolf in any form in countless years. Perhaps he finally died in battle as he wished, only finding peace in his own destruction, or perhaps he saw the weakness in his own tribe and chose to abandon it. Whatever the case, Bloodied Wolf is gone, and the Destroyer's Blood, believing themselves the heirs to his duty, have taken on new patrons, the Maeljin, which has ultimately been the killing blow in thoroughly corrupting what claimed to be the 'purest' of tribes, as there is no purity in the hunt of the Destroyer's Blood. A wolf does not hunt out of malice or vice, but out of necessity, taking what they need to sustain themselves and the world around them. The werewolves of the Destroyer's Blood hunt because they can, and in thorough excess at that, reveling in the atrocities which they commit.<br /> They draw in a surprising amount of members, which is necessary, considering their swift rates of membership turnover on account of their violent lifestyle. Part of this is just a matter of rapid reproduction, as killing is not their only avenue of vice any longer. However, the sad truth of it is that they also embody what may be the darkest thoughts in every Uratha. With the incredible power and lack of control in their form, werewolves are able to commit terrible things that they could never get away with as mere mortals, without compunction except for the laws of flesh and spirit binding them. Those who cannot resist such temptations are often drawn in to join the Destroyer's Blood, and continue along their warpath of destruction, of both their self and the world around them.</i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3094-leafy","name":"Leafy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3094_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}